{"cuisine/dominican-republic-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/dominican-republic-cuisine","n":"Dominican Republic cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Dominica","intro":[["Dominican cuisine is made up of Spanish, Indigenous Taíno, Middle Eastern, and African influences. The most recent influences in Dominican cuisine are from the British West Indies and China."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dominican-republic-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominican_Republic_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Dominican Cooking","ref":"site/dominicancooking-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dominicancooking.com","note":"Long-running Dominican recipe site run by Clara González.","url":"https://www.dominicancooking.com/start","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"My Dominican Kitchen","ref":"site/mydominicankitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mydominicankitchen.com","note":"Blog of Dominican home recipes.","url":"https://mydominicankitchen.com/category/recipes/main-dishes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Dominican Kitchen: Homestyle Recipes That Celebrate the Flavors, Traditions, and Culture of the Dominican Republic","ref":"cookbook/the-dominican-kitchen-homestyle-recipes-that-celebrate-the-flavors-traditions-and-culture-of-the-dominican-republic","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Vanessa Mota","note":"Dominican home-style recipes with background on the island's food culture.","url":"https://www.quarto.com/books/9781631068874/the-dominican-kitchen","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781631068874"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Yaroa","dish/yaroa"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2a/BanderaDominicana01.jpg/500px-BanderaDominicana01.jpg","w":500,"h":259,"by":"Asalas03","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BanderaDominicana01.jpg"}},"cuisine/hazara-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hazara-cuisine","n":"Hazara cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Afghan cuisine","cuisine/afghan-cuisine"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Afghanistan","intro":[["Hazara cuisine or Hazaragi cuisine refers to the food and cuisine of the Hazara people in Afghanistan and western Pakistan (Balochistan province). The food of the Hazara people is strongly influenced by Central Asian, South Asian cuisines and shares similarities with neighboring regional cuisines in Afghanistan and Central Asia. However, there are certain dishes, culinary methods and styles of cooking that are unique to the Hazara people."],["The Hazara people have a hospitable dining etiquette. In Hazaragi culture, it is customary to prepare special food for guests, and to honor them with the best seats during meal times. Most Hazaras eat food with their hands, as opposed to using cutlery and dining utensils such as forks, knives, or spoons. The diet of the Hazara people is largely based on the intake of high-protein foods such as meats and dairy products. They use large amounts of oil in their cooking. A typical Hazara meal/dining course normally consists of cooking one type of food or dish, rather than a wide selection. However, in large formal gatherings or during the presence of guests, a variety of foods may be cooked in the household."],["Hazara cuisine is largely centred on breads. There are three main types of breads consumed by Hazara people:"],["Tawa bread, baked on hot plates"],["Tandoor bread, which is baked on a sunken oven known as the \"tandoor\""],["Nan-buta bread, a thick, brick-sized bread."],["Rice is less frequent in rural Hazara cuisine due to its expense. Tea is a popular beverage among the Hazara people. Fruits and vegetables are only consumed when in season."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hazara-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazara_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Afghan cuisine","cuisine/afghan-cuisine"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Afghan cuisine","cuisine/afghan-cuisine"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/hainan-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hainan-cuisine","n":"Hainan cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Min cuisine","cuisine/fujian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Hainan cuisine, or Hainanese cuisine, is derived from the cooking styles of the peoples of Hainan Province in China. The food is lighter, less oily, and more mildly seasoned than that of the Chinese mainland. Seafood predominates the menu, as prawn, crab, and freshwater and ocean fish are widely available."],["Congee, mantou and baozi are eaten for breakfast, with a noodle dish also being widely eaten. This consists of fine, vermicelli-type noodles with various toppings and gravy. Along with lunch and dinner, late night outdoor barbecue dishes are also served."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hainan-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hainan_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Madame Huang's Kitchen: Henan cuisine","ref":"site/carolynjphillips-blogspot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"carolynjphillips.blogspot.com","note":"Recipe posts on Hainan dishes from Carolyn Phillips' blog, Madame Huang's Kitchen.","url":"https://carolynjphillips.blogspot.com/search/label/Hainan%20cuisine","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"All Under Heaven: Recipes from the 35 Cuisines of China","ref":"cookbook/all-under-heaven-recipes-from-the-35-cuisines-of-china","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Carolyn Phillips, 2016","note":"Regional survey of Chinese cooking that includes a section on Hainan dishes.","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/534780/all-under-heaven-by-carolyn-phillips/9781607749820/","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781607749820"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Min cuisine","cuisine/fujian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d4/Hainanese_chicken_rice.jpg/500px-Hainanese_chicken_rice.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Misaochan","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hainanese_chicken_rice.jpg","of":"Hainanese chicken rice"}},"cuisine/syrian-jewish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/syrian-jewish-cuisine","n":"Syrian Jewish cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Syria","intro":[["Syrian Jewish cuisine is the cuisine of Syrian Jews. Although almost all Jewish people left Syria by 2016, their cuisine has been preserved in books and family recipes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"syrian-jewish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syrian_Jewish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Aromas of Aleppo: The Legendary Cuisine of Syrian Jews","ref":"cookbook/aromas-of-aleppo-the-legendary-cuisine-of-syrian-jews","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Poopa Dweck and Michael J. Cohen, 2007","note":"Recipes and traditions of the Aleppo Jewish community and its Brooklyn diaspora.","url":"https://www.harpercollins.ca/9780060888183/aromas-of-aleppo","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780060888183"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Syrian cuisine","cuisine/syrian-cuisine"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/aymara-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/aymara-foodways","n":"Aymara foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Altiplano around Lake Titicaca, Bolivia and Peru","names":"Aymara","intro":[["Aymara families farm the Bolivian highlands, 3,000 to 4,000 meters up, where the sun is strong and the nights freeze. After the ",["potato","ingredient/potato"]," harvest, they sort the crop three ways: to eat soon, to plant next year, and to turn into ",["Chuño","ingredient/chuno"],". On frosty nights in June and July those potatoes are left out to freeze, then trodden to squeeze out the water, peeled and dried hard in the sun, and they can keep for decades. Tunta starts the same way but is soaked in a running river, which turns it white."],["On Lake Titicaca, Aymara fishers have eaten karachi, a tiny native fish, for thousands of years, fried with salt or added to soups, and trade it for ",["Quinoa","ingredient/quinoa"]," and potatoes on market days. Overfishing and trout, brought into the lake in the 1930s, now threaten it."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Cultural Survival, Chuño, a symbol of food sovereignty and ancestral food preservation","url":"https://www.culturalsurvival.org/news/chuno-symbol-food-sovereignty-and-ancestral-food-preservation"},{"name":"Culture Trip, Lake Titicaca's pre-Incan fishing culture is in danger of being lost","url":"https://theculturetrip.com/south-america/peru/articles/lake-titicacas-pre-incan-fishing-culture-is-in-danger-of-being-lost"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"aymara-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Inca cuisine","cuisine/inca-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"dish/beach-pizza":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/beach-pizza","n":"Beach pizza","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Cuisine of New England","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Beach pizza is a style of pizza popular in the coastal communities of New England north of Boston, particularly in northeastern Massachusetts and coastal New Hampshire."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-beach-pizza"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beach_pizza"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Cuisine of New England","cuisine/cuisine-of-new-england"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Yankee Magazine: Chowder Recipes","ref":"site/newengland-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newengland.com","note":"Recipes from Yankee, the long-running New England regional magazine.","url":"https://newengland.com/food/soups-stews-chowders/favorite-chowder-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"magazine"}]},{"h":"More from Cuisine of New England","links":[["Boston baked beans","dish/boston-baked-beans"],["Clam cake","dish/clam-cake"],["Parker House roll","dish/parker-house-roll"],["Thanksgiving sandwich","dish/thanksgiving-sandwich"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ae/Tripoli%27s_in_Salisbury%2C_Massachusetts.jpg/500px-Tripoli%27s_in_Salisbury%2C_Massachusetts.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"uzi978","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripoli's_in_Salisbury%2C_Massachusetts.jpg"}},"ingredient/paste":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/paste","n":"Paste","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A food paste is a semi-liquid colloidal suspension, emulsion, or aggregation used in food preparation or eaten directly as a spread. Pastes are often highly spicy or aromatic, are often prepared well in advance of actual usage, and are often made into a preserve for future use. Common pastes are some fruit preserves, curry pastes, and nut pastes. Purées are food pastes made from already cooked ingredients."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-paste"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paste_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Canapé","dish/canape"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Canapé","dish/canape"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Cracker","ingredient/cracker"],["Puff pastry","ingredient/puff-pastry"],["Spread","ingredient/spread"],["Toast","ingredient/toast"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2c/Shrimp.paste-Sambal.Belacan-01.jpg/500px-Shrimp.paste-Sambal.Belacan-01.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"by daxiang stef","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shrimp.paste-Sambal.Belacan-01.jpg"}},"dish/chow-chow-food":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/chow-chow-food","n":"Chow-chow (food)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from American cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Chow-chow (also spelled chowchow or chow chow) is a pickled dish popular in North America whose origins are unclear. Some suggest an origin in the American South, while other sources suggest it originated in Canada and was brought south by the Acadians who migrated to the American South after being expelled from the Maritimes in the mid-1700s; another theory is that it originated from Chinese railway workers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-chow-chow-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chow-chow_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]},{"h":"More from American cuisine","links":[["Angel food cake","dish/angel-food-cake"],["Banana bread","dish/banana-bread"],["Banana pudding","dish/banana-pudding"],["Barbecue sauce","dish/barbecue-sauce"],["Beer cake","dish/beer-cake"],["Better than sex cake","dish/better-than-sex-cake"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Blooming onion","dish/blooming-onion"],["Brown Betty (dessert)","dish/brown-betty-dessert"],["Brunswick stew","dish/brunswick-stew"],["Buffalo wing","dish/buffalo-wing"],["Butter cake","dish/butter-cake"],["Caesar salad","dish/caesar-salad"],["Caramel apple","dish/caramel-apple"],["Carbonara","dish/carbonara"],["Cheesesteak","dish/cheesesteak"],["Chicago-style hot dog","dish/chicago-style-hot-dog"],["Chicken nugget","dish/chicken-nugget"],["Chiffon cake","dish/chiffon-cake"],["Chili con carne","dish/chili-con-carne"],["Chimichanga","dish/chimichanga"],["Chowder","dish/chowder"],["Cioppino","dish/cioppino"],["Clam chowder","dish/clam-chowder"],["Clam dip","dish/clam-dip"],["Club sandwich","dish/club-sandwich"],["Cobb salad","dish/cobb-salad"],["Cobbler (food)","dish/cobbler-food"],["Cook's Corner","dish/cook-s-corner"],["Crab cake","dish/crab-cake"],["Croffle","dish/croffle"],["Dagwood Bumstead","dish/dagwood-bumstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"],["Fritter","dish/fritter"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/6a/Chow_Chow.JPG/500px-Chow_Chow.JPG","w":500,"h":430,"by":"Geoff","lic"