Armenian cookbooks.","url":"https://www.thearmeniankitchen.com/classic-armenian-recipes-another/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"My Albanian Food","ref":"site/myalbanianfood-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myalbanianfood.com","note":"Albanian home recipes shared by an Albanian cook.","url":"https://www.myalbanianfood.com/?p=936","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Dimitra's Dishes","ref":"site/dimitrasdishes-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dimitrasdishes.com","note":"Dimitra Alfieris's video channel and blog focused on classic Greek home cooking.","url":"https://dimitrasdishes.com","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Ozlem's Turkish Table","ref":"site/ozlemsturkishtable-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"ozlemsturkishtable.com","note":"Turkish-born cook and teacher Ozlem Warren's blog of home-style Turkish recipes.","url":"https://ozlemsturkishtable.com/tag/turkish-recipes/page/9","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Armenian cuisine","links":[["Alani (dried fruit)","dish/alani-dried-fruit"],["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bagharj","dish/bagharj"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khorovats","dish/khorovats"],["Lavash","dish/lavash"],["Matnakash","dish/matnakash"],["Mikado cake","dish/mikado-cake"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Pirozhki","dish/pirozhki"],["Spas (soup)","dish/spas-soup"],["Tjvjik (food)","dish/tjvjik-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/7b/%C4%B0mam_bay%C4%B1ld%C4%B1_AvL.JPG/500px-%C4%B0mam_bay%C4%B1ld%C4%B1_AvL.JPG","w":500,"h":333,"by":"AlexanderVanLoon","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%B0mam_bay%C4%B1ld%C4%B1_AvL.JPG"}},"ingredient/cumian":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cumian","n":"Cumian","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Cumian are thick Chinese noodles made from wheat flour and water. Two types of Chinese noodles are called cumian. One is Shanghai-style, thick in diameter, used in Shanghai fried noodles."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cumian"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumian"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Korean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/korean-chinese-cuisine",[["Jjajangmyeon","dish/jjajangmyeon"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Sweet bean sauce","ingredient/sweet-bean-sauce"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/43/Pan-Fried_Shanghai_Noodles.jpg/500px-Pan-Fried_Shanghai_Noodles.jpg","w":500,"h":625,"by":"Sharon Chen","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pan-Fried_Shanghai_Noodles.jpg"}},"ingredient/jaggery":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/jaggery","n":"Jaggery","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Jaggery is a traditional non-centrifugal cane sugar consumed in South Asia, Southeast Asia, North America, Central America, Brazil and Africa. It is a concentrated product of cane juice and often date or palm sap  without separation of the molasses and crystals, and can vary from golden brown to dark brown in colour. It contains up to 50% sucrose, up to 20% invert sugars, and up to 20% moisture, with the remainder made up of other insoluble matter, such as wood ash, proteins, and bagasse fibres. Jaggery is very similar to muscovado, an important sweetener in Portuguese, British and French cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-jaggery"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaggery"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Rajasthani cuisine","cuisine/rajasthani-cuisine",[["Katt bafla","dish/katt-bafla"]]],["Thai cuisine","cuisi